# Connecting a computer

<% if @request_auth_available %>
This host authenticates the initial request automatically. Do not run browser
connection setup or create a personal token. Follow the primer's mint command.
<% else %>
A browser login does not authenticate your local tools. Connect the computer
once, then reuse its private credential to mint a session token for each run.

## First connection

Download the helper from this CoPlan host and run it with Ruby:

```bash
mkdir -p ~/.config/coplan
curl -fsS <%= Shellwords.escape("#{@base}/agent-tools/coplan-connect") %> -o ~/.config/coplan/coplan-connect
ruby ~/.config/coplan/coplan-connect --base <%= Shellwords.escape(@base) %> --name 'Local computer'
```

The helper prints a short-lived approval link and a matching code. It opens the
browser when it can. Otherwise, give your principal the link and code. Let them
sign in, check the code, and choose **Connect this computer**. Do not approve
for them. Opening the link does not grant access. No webhook is needed.

The helper polls for at most 10 minutes. When approved, it saves the personal
credential directly to `~/.config/coplan/credentials.json` with file mode 0600.
It never prints the credential. The browser never receives it. Do not read that
file into a tool response, chat, skill, source repository, or command argument.
Load its `token` into a process variable without printing it, as in the primer.
Keep this helper installed so you can reconnect after revocation.

Use `--no-open` when a local browser is unavailable. Use `--replace` when this
host's saved credential is revoked. Reconnection still needs human approval.
The helper refuses to overwrite another host's file. For another host, choose
`--credentials /path/to/a/separate/private/credentials.json`, and load that file
for that host's mint command. HTTPS is required except on loopback addresses.

## After connection

Fetch <<%= @base %>/agent-instructions> and mint your own session token. Use
that session for your run. Do not create a new personal credential for each
agent or turn. The personal credential represents the computer's access;
its child sessions represent individual agent runs.

Your principal can revoke the personal token at **Settings → Personal API
tokens**. That also revokes every child session. For manual setup, they can
still create a personal token there and store it in the same JSON format.
<% end %>
